# Pipeline efficiency (time ↔ space)

When designing or changing multi-stage scientific/ML pipelines, optimize for **avoiding accidental recomputation**, not for micro-optimizing hot loops.

## Prefer unlock artifacts over redo

- If a **small intermediate artifact** would unlock a later stage, prefer saving it over redoing expensive work (full retrain, full re-inference, full re-encode).
- Explicitly consider and suggest **time ↔ space** tradeoffs: wall time vs disk, recompute vs store, resume vs restart.

## Batch / cluster jobs

- Account for **wall-time limits**, **disk quota**, and **resume / skip-existing** behavior.
- Design stages so a failed or partial run can continue without replaying completed expensive steps.

## Avoid O(stages) × full redo

- Do **not** accidentally force **O(stages) × full retrain / re-inference** when a later stage only needs a summary.
- Save **cheap summaries** (means, scalars, small maps, metadata) when a downstream stage needs them.

### Example (encode vs pooled maps)

Per-fold encode/train is expensive; a later pooled-map stage may only need tiny **fold-mean** summaries. Prefer writing those once during encode over refitting ridge (or re-running encode) later just to rebuild them. Same pattern applies beyond LOO: any leave-one-out / CV / multi-seed stage should persist what the next stage actually consumes.

## What not to do by default

- Do **not** micro-optimize hot loops unless profiling shows a real bottleneck.
- **Do** flag recomputation hazards, repeated full I/O passes, and disk-quota risks when proposing pipeline changes.
